      ],
      note: 'A weather-tight shell is not a finished room. Walls, ceilings, flooring, electric, insulation, HVAC, plumbing and exterior paint are separate decisions unless specifically included in the written scope.',
    },
    {
      type: 'cards', id: 'payments', title: 'Payment Timing Should Match the Project', nav: 'Deposits & Payments',
      lead: 'A small storage building and a customized studio may use different payment structures. What matters is that the timing is explained and documented before work begins.',
      items: [
        { title: 'What a deposit is for', text: 'Scheduling commitment, material planning, ordering and project readiness. Its purpose and treatment belong in writing.' },
        { title: 'What a deposit is not', text: 'A substitute for scope clarity. Know the model, size, features, upgrades, exclusions and timing first.' },
        { title: 'Progress or milestone payments', text: 'Larger projects may tie payments to order confirmation, material readiness, build start or substantial completion.' },
        { title: 'Final payment', text: 'Tied to completion, cleanup, the walkthrough and any agreed punch-list items.' },
        { title: 'Payment method', text: 'Confirm accepted methods, timing, receipts and any fees.' },
        { title: 'Allowances', text: 'An estimate for a selection not yet final. The scope should say what happens if the final choice costs more or less.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      type: 'cards', id: 'red-flags', title: 'Budget Mistakes Usually Begin Before the Deposit', nav: 'Budget Red Flags',
