    { q: 'How much deposit is normal for a backyard building?', a: 'It varies by project size, materials and schedule. What matters is that the amount, what it reserves, how it applies to the project and what happens if the scope changes are confirmed in writing before you pay.' },
    { q: 'Should I pay a deposit before the scope is written?', a: 'No. Understand the model, size, features, upgrades, exclusions, site assumptions, payment timing and change process first.' },
    { q: 'When are payments usually due?', a: 'It depends on the project. Smaller projects may use a deposit and final payment; larger or customized projects may use documented milestones set before work begins.' },
    { q: 'What causes surprise costs?', a: 'Unclear site preparation, access limits, drainage, foundation assumptions, late upgrades, open allowances, permit or HOA issues, and verbal changes never written into the scope.' },
    { q: 'Can I add upgrades later to control the budget now?', a: 'Some, like shelving, workbenches and certain interior finishes. Foundation, framing, roof, openings, electrical readiness, insulation and plumbing readiness are better decided before construction.' },
